/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A node is a point where traffic is added, dropped, switched or regenerated.
   - A lightpath is an end-to-end connection that uses one wavelength on every link of its path and is not converted to electronics in between; some books call it a wavelength path.
   - A transparent network keeps the signal optical end to end; impairments add up along the path (see Signal degradation in optical fibers), so the reach is limited.
   - Common physical topologies are mesh, ring, star and bus.
   - The public telecommunication network has three tiers.
   - Traffic crosses these networks in two transmission modes.
   - An optical node does four jobs.
   - Protection reserves capacity before a failure.
   - A broadcast-and-select network sends every wavelength to every node, and each receiver filters the one it wants.
*/
