/* book exam traps: Physical topology is not the lightpath topology. A ring of fiber can carry a full mesh of lightpaths, so do not copy the fiber graph into the logical graph. | Count a bidirectional lightpath once per pair, N(N − 1)/2, and give its traffic per direction. Counting each node’s demands separately gives 30 demands and doubles the traffic. | Dedicated 1+1 on separate wavelengths doubles the wavelengths used; 1:N shares one backup among N working paths and does not. | A lightpath needs the same wavelength on every link unless a converter is present. A free wavelength on one link is not enough. | 1+1 and 1:1 both have a backup, but in 1+1 the signal is always sent on both paths; only a 1:1 backup can carry extra traffic while healthy. */
