/* book: lead: SONET in North America and SDH elsewhere define one synchronous frame that carries many low-rate channels on a single optical line. One master clock and byte interleaving let any channel be added or dropped without taking the whole signal apart. The frame repeats every 125 µs, so every rate is an exact multiple of 51.84 Mb/s. Telephone companies built voice, leased-line and protected-ring networks */
/* book Figure 6.6.1: The STS-1 frame of the worked example: 3 columns of transport overhead (section overhead in rows 1 to 3, line overhead in rows 4 to 9 with the pointer bytes H1, H2, H3 in row 4) and an 87-column SPE that starts with the path overhead column. 810 bytes 8000 times a second give 51.84 Mb/s. */
