/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - The older plesiochronous digital hierarchy (PDH) let every multiplexer run on its own, nearly equal clock and used bit stuffing to make up the difference.
   - The SONET building block is the STS-1 electrical signal at 51.84 Mb/s; its optical form is OC-1.
   - A pointer in the line overhead marks where the synchronous payload envelope (SPE) starts inside the frame.
   - The STS-1 frame is a rectangle of 90 columns × 9 rows = 810 bytes, sent row by row.
   - R is the bit rate in b/s; 8 bits per byte; 8000 frames per second.
   - So the SPE carries 783 × 64 000 = 50.112 Mb/s.
   - The STM-1 frame is 270 columns × 9 rows = 2430 bytes in the same 125 µs.
   - SONET has four layers.
   - An add-drop multiplexer (ADM) uses the fixed byte positions: it drops selected tributaries, inserts new traffic into the same positions and passes the rest, with no full demultiplexing.
   - Jitter is short-term variation of the clock phase, at 10 Hz and above; wander is slow drift below 10 Hz.
*/
